WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.333
Can multiplication help you solve a division, or uncover a hidden number?

00:00:04.983 --> 00:00:10.064
Today we are connecting inverse operations, estimates and real situations.

00:00:10.284 --> 00:00:16.099
We will identify equal groups, find a missing value, and check whether an answer has the right size.

00:00:16.749 --> 00:00:21.911
Our learning goal is to use inverse operations, estimates and equal-group models.

00:00:22.561 --> 00:00:24.318
Success criteria.

00:00:24.538 --> 00:00:28.366
I can find a missing factor using an inverse relationship.

00:00:28.586 --> 00:00:31.754
I can estimate before finding an exact total.

00:00:31.974 --> 00:00:35.054
I can explain an answer with its meaning and unit.

00:00:35.704 --> 00:00:40.491
Multiplication and division describe the same equal-group relationship.

00:00:40.711 --> 00:00:46.618
If groups times group size gives the total, dividing the total by one known factor finds the other.

00:00:46.838 --> 00:00:49.777
An estimate provides a separate size check.

00:00:49.997 --> 00:00:52.871
Three equal groups of four make twelve.

00:00:53.091 --> 00:00:56.844
Sharing twelve into three equal groups gives four in each.

00:00:57.494 --> 00:01:02.138
A missing number times twenty-four equals one thousand six hundred and eighty.

00:01:02.358 --> 00:01:05.900
Divide the total by twenty-four to find the missing number.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:10.681
Seventy groups of twenty-four make one thousand six hundred and eighty.

00:01:10.901 --> 00:01:15.737
Substitute seventy into the original sentence to verify the relationship.

00:01:16.387 --> 00:01:21.437
A shop has twenty-one boxes with three hundred and ninety-eight pencils in each.

00:01:21.657 --> 00:01:26.392
Estimate using twenty boxes of four hundred: about eight thousand pencils.

00:01:27.042 --> 00:01:32.540
The exact calculation is three hundred and ninety-eight times twenty plus one more group.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:40.080
Seven thousand nine hundred and sixty plus three hundred and ninety-eight gives eight thousand three hundred and fifty-eight pencils.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:45.832
An estimate is a useful check, but do not use eight thousand as the exact total.

00:01:46.052 --> 00:01:50.779
Keep twenty-one groups of three hundred and ninety-eight for the real calculation.

00:01:50.999 --> 00:01:55.434
An inverse check uses the exact values, not the rounded ones.

00:01:56.084 --> 00:02:00.781
A missing number times eighteen equals one thousand four hundred and forty.

00:02:01.001 --> 00:02:02.155
What is it?

00:02:06.155 --> 00:02:11.585
Eighty times eighteen equals one thousand four hundred and forty, so the missing number is eighty.

00:02:12.235 --> 00:02:18.816
Identify the equal groups, use an inverse operation for missing values, and estimate before calculating.

00:02:19.036 --> 00:02:23.154
Check by substitution and state what the result means in the problem.

00:02:23.374 --> 00:02:26.677
Choose a model and explain why your strategy works.
